course: biology
lesson: 86
title: The monkey house and the lemurs of Madagascar
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ring-tailed lemurs sit in the morning sun with their arms out like children doing yoga, warming their bellies after a cold night; in the wild they live only on Madagascar, an island off Africa.
explain: Lemurs are not monkeys but an older branch of the primate family, and in King Julien's real groups it is the females, not a king, who are in charge and eat first.
explain: In the monkey house a spider monkey hangs by its tail alone like a fifth hand and eats with both hands, a howler monkey booms so loud it fills the building, and a capuchin cracks a nut with a stone like a hammer.
explain: Old cartoons love a monkey slipping on a banana skin, but real monkeys peel bananas neatly and nobody has seen one slip; the joke is for people.
fact: Ring-tailed lemurs sunbathe in the morning to warm up, and wild lemurs live only on Madagascar.
fact: Male ring-tailed lemurs rub scent from their wrists onto their tails and wave them at rivals in a «stink fight»; lemurs are not monkeys.
fact: In ring-tailed lemur groups the females lead, eat first and win most disputes, and many kinds of lemur are among the most endangered primates.
fact: Many monkeys of Central and South America have a gripping, or prehensile, tail that holds branches like a fifth hand, with ridged skin under the tip; no African or Asian monkeys have one.
fact: Golden lion tamarins from Brazil fell to about 200 in the wild in the 1970s; zoos bred them and returned some to protected forest, and there are now a few thousand.
fact: Tamarins and marmosets usually have twins, and the father and older brothers and sisters carry the babies most of the time.
fact: Howler monkeys are among the loudest land animals; a big hollow bone in the throat makes their dawn calls echo, grow louder and carry for several kilometres.
fact: Male mandrills have the brightest faces of any monkey, bright blue and red, and the brightest belong to the strongest males; Rafiki in The Lion King is drawn like a mandrill crossed with a baboon.
fact: Visitors' crisps, bread and sweets, full of salt, fat and sugar, make zoo monkeys ill, because they need a careful diet of leaves, vegetables, insects and pellets.
fact: Banana skins are slippery because of a gel on the inside; scientists who measured it won the joke Ig Nobel Prize in 2014.
fact: Wild capuchin monkeys in Brazil crack hard nuts with stones on a flat rock, and some of their stone tools are about 3,000 years old.
fact: Sifakas are lemurs built for leaping between trees, so on the ground they bounce sideways with their arms up, like dancers.
exercise: Why do ring-tailed lemurs sit with arms out facing the sun? | To warm their bellies after a cold night | To dry their wet fur after a long swim in the river | To ask the keepers for food | To show they are the leader
exercise: Where do lemurs live in the wild? | Only on Madagascar | All over mainland Africa | In the rainforests of Brazil | In the forests of India and China
exercise: What is a lemur stink fight? | Males wave tails rubbed with wrist scent at rivals | Lemurs throw handfuls of dung at each other until one runs off | Lemurs roll in mud to smell bad | Two lemurs fight with their teeth
exercise: Are lemurs monkeys? | No, they are an older branch of primates | Yes, they are small monkeys that came from Africa | Yes, they are baby monkeys | No, they are a kind of cat
exercise: Who leads a group of ring-tailed lemurs? | The females | A king, like King Julien | The oldest male | The zoo keeper
exercise: What can a spider monkey's tail do? | Grip branches like a fifth hand | Sting enemies like a scorpion's tail | Help it swim fast | Nothing, it just hangs down
exercise: Where do monkeys with gripping tails come from? | Central and South America | Africa | Asia | Madagascar
exercise: How many golden lion tamarins were left in the wild in the 1970s? | About 200 | Only about 2 | About 20,000 | About 2 million
exercise: How did zoos help golden lion tamarins? | They bred them and returned some to protected forest | They taught them to live in cities and parks among people | They moved them all to Africa | They kept them all forever in cages
exercise: Who carries tamarin babies most of the time? | The father and older brothers and sisters | Only the mother, until they are grown | Nobody, babies walk at once | The grandmother alone
exercise: Why are howler monkeys so loud? | A big hollow bone in the throat makes the sound echo | They have two extra lungs that push out much more air than ours | They shout through hollow trees | Their ears are very small
exercise: What does a male mandrill's bright face show? | How strong and important he is | That he is ill with a bad fever | That he is still very young indeed | That he is hungry and wants food
exercise: Why must visitors never feed the monkeys? | Snacks full of salt, fat and sugar make them ill | Monkeys hate all human food and will throw it back at you | Feeding makes monkeys grow too big | Monkeys are allergic to paper bags
exercise: Do monkeys really slip on banana skins? | No, they peel them neatly; the slip is a cartoon joke | Yes, they slip on them every day in the zoo, it is very common | Yes, but only on wet days | Only young monkeys do
exercise: How does a capuchin open a hard nut? | It bangs it with a stone on a flat rock | It soaks it in the water for a whole week until it goes soft | It gives it to a parrot | It bites it with its back teeth
exercise: Why does a sifaka hop sideways on the ground? | Its legs are built for leaping between trees | It is dancing to make the visitors laugh and clap | One of its legs is shorter | The ground is too hot to walk on
test: In Madagascar the cartoon, King Julien rules the lemurs. What would you fix? | Real ring-tailed lemur groups are led by females | Nothing, lemurs always have a king who wears a crown of leaves | Real lemurs live in New York | Real lemurs are led by the youngest male
test: «5 spider monkeys hanging off one branch, how many arms and tails is that» | 10 arms and 5 tails | 20 arms and 5 tails | 10 arms and no tails | 25 arms and 10 tails
test: A tamarin mum has twins, and her neighbour has twins too. How many babies are there? | 4 | 2 | 3 | 6
test: «lemur's just a monkey from Madagascar, same thing» What is right? | Lemurs are an older branch of primates, not monkeys | Yes, all primates are monkeys, so lemurs are monkeys too | Lemurs are a kind of cat | Lemurs are baby monkeys that grow up
test: Rafiki in The Lion King looks like a mandrill crossed with a baboon. What is true about real mandrills? | Males have bright blue and red faces that show rank | They are white all over with pink faces, like a snow monkey | They live in Madagascar with lemurs | They carry a stick and do magic
test: «chuck the monkey my crisps, he's starving look» What is right? | No, crisps are full of salt and fat and make monkeys ill | Yes, monkeys love crisps and need the salt to stay healthy | Yes, but only salty ones | Only if you give him a drink as well
test: What would happen if the golden lion tamarins were bred in zoos but their forest kept being cut down? | There would be nowhere safe in the wild to return them | They would quickly learn to live without trees, on the ground | The zoos would run out of space at once | Nothing, zoo tamarins never need the wild
test: Why would an African monkey never hang by its tail like a spider monkey? | No African or Asian monkey has a gripping tail | African monkeys have no tails at all | African monkeys are too heavy for trees | African monkeys only live on the ground
test: A cartoon monkey slips on a banana skin and flies across the room. What did scientists really find? | The skin has a slippery gel, but monkeys peel bananas neatly | Banana skins are not slippery at all | Monkeys slip on them every day in zoos | Bananas grow already peeled
test: A friend says only apes and people use tools. What would you say? | Capuchins crack nuts with stones, so monkeys use tools too | Right, no monkey has ever used a tool | Only birds and people use tools | Monkeys use tools but only in cartoons
test: 3 ring-tailed lemurs and 2 sifakas sunbathe. how many legs and tails, quick | 20 legs and 5 tails | 10 legs and 5 tails | 20 legs and 10 tails | 15 legs and 5 tails
test: «why's that howler so loud, he's only small» What is right? | A hollow bone in his throat makes the sound echo and grow | He has a microphone hidden in the tree, lol | He is much bigger than he looks | Small animals are always the loudest
exam: Identify the location to which the lemurs are confined in the wild. | The island of Madagascar | The whole of continental Africa | The rainforests of South America | The whole Indian subcontinent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the social organisation of the ring-tailed lemur? | Groups are led by females, who feed first and win most disputes | Groups are governed by a single hereditary male ruler and his sons | Each lemur lives in solitude | Groups are led by the youngest male
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the prehensile tail. | It occurs in some American monkeys, but in no African or Asian monkey | It occurs in all monkeys without exception | It occurs only in the lemurs of Madagascar | It occurs only in the great apes
exam: It is given that approximately two hundred golden lion tamarins remained in the wild during the 1970s. Determine the principal reason for their subsequent recovery. | Breeding in zoos and the return of animals to protected forest | The relocation of the entire population to the forests of Africa | The provision of human food by tourists | The cessation of all scientific study
